%[text] %[text:anchor:H_48639868] ## What is pressure?
%[text] 
%[text] Pressure is a measure of the force a fluid exerts on the wall of its container. On a microscopic scale, the fluid particles impact the container wall and bounce back from it. Because the fluid has many particles, all those discrete impacts can be added together over time. The measure of the intensity of those impacts is the pressure, denoted   $p${"altText":"p"} or $P${"altText":"P"}. 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:9a8f) **Try.** Run the following function to visualize fluid particle impact on a surface.
  %[control:button:898a]{"position":[1,2]}
PlotPressure
%[text] Pressure is a scalar quantity in the whole fluid domain and is dimensionally equivalent to a force per surface unit. The total resulting force on a surface can be estimated using the following relationships:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_39947CDA] $F = P\\times S\n${"altText":"F = P\times S "}
%[text] Where $F${"altText":"F"} is the magnitude of the force, $P${"altText":"P"} is the pressure, and $S${"altText":"S"} on which the pressure is applied.
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5004B432] ### Absolute vs. Relative pressure
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Balloon with annotation for the exterior (P\_atm) and interior (P\_in) pressure](text:image:160b)
%[text] Take a regular rubber air balloon. The pressure inside the balloon   $P\_{in}${"altText":"P\_{in}"} is superior to the pressure outside it  $P\_{atm}${"altText":"P\_{atm}"}, giving it its shape. Three forces are acting on the balloon:
%[text] 1. The atmospheric pressure exerts a force toward the center of the balloon.
%[text] 2. The inner pressure exerts a force from the center of the balloon outward.
%[text] 3. The surface tension of the rubber along the balloon’s surface. \
%[text] The force in the balloon must balance each other; therefore, the surface tension force is a function of the pressure difference between the inside and outside of the balloon. We define the relative pressure such that:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_\\text{relative}=P - P\_\\text{reference}${"altText":"P\_\text{relative}=P - P\_\text{reference}"}
%[text] The pressure $P${"altText":"P"} represents any pressure and is referred to as absolute pressure so that:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_\\text{relative}=P\_\\text{absolute} - P\_\\text{reference}${"altText":"P\_\text{relative}=P\_\text{absolute} - P\_\text{reference}"}
%[text] The reference pressure is chosen to be the atmospheric pressure so that the relative pressure of the atmosphere is zero and the relative pressure of the balloon is $P\_{in} -P\_{atm}${"altText":"P\_{in} -P\_{atm}"}.
%[text] **Remark:** the most common reference pressure is the atmospheric pressure (1 atm = 101325 Pa), but we could use any arbitrary reference.
%[text] Pressure measurements are often relative measurements. For example, a bike tire pump will show zero pressure if your tire is completely deflated. It does not mean that the absolute pressure inside the tire is zero but that the air inside is at the same pressure as the atmosphere.
%[text] **Remark:** because absolute pressure is a macroscopic measure of the fluid impact on a surface $P\_\\text{absolute} \\geq 0${"altText":"P\_\text{absolute} \geq 0"}, there is either no or some impact, but a negative number of impacts does not make physical sense. However, because the relative pressure is an offset of pressure, negative relative pressure is possible.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:7944) **Exercise.** Click the following figure to identify the different pressures and measurements presented.
  %[control:button:7e94]{"position":[1,2]}
IdentifyPressure
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4A598671] ### Units
%[text] 
%[text] The pressure reference unit in the standard unit system is the Pascal (Pa), named after the French mathematician and physicist [Blaise Pascal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blaise_Pascal). However, engineers and scientists use many units depending on their field and application. While an engineer specializing in hydraulics applications might use "meter of water" as the unit of choice, an aerodynamicist might use psi (pound per square inches) or Pa.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:4ea7) **Try.** Run the following code to create a unit system and get information about Pascals.
units = symunit;
unitInfo(units.Pa)
%%
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:011a) **Try.** Following the prompt from `unitInfo`, explore all units of pressure available.
unitInfo("Pressure")
%[text] Some of the most common units of pressure are:
%[text] - mmHg (millimeter of mercury) is used in medicine to measure blood pressure; for example, a blood pressure of 120/80 means that the systolic (while the heart pump) pressure is 120 mmHg and the diastolic (while the heart is at rest in between beats) pressure is 80 mmHg.
%[text] - mH2O (meter of water) is used to design large hydraulic systems such as pipelines or hydropower plants.
%[text] - atm (atmosphere) corresponds to sea-level atmospheric pressure (1 atm = 101325 Pa). It has been used in chemistry as standard pressure.
%[text] - bar (bar) closest multiple of Pascal to the atmosphere (1 bar = 100000 Pa) is now used to define the standard pressure in chemistry.
%[text] - psi is commonly used as an empirical unit of pressure. \
%[text] You should get familiar with some of the most common units you might encounter in fluid mechanics: Pa, bar, atm, and psi.
%%
%[text] You should get familiar with some of the most common units you might encounter in fluid mechanics: Pa, bar, atm, and psi.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:508f) **Exercise.** Use [`unitConversionFactor`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/unitconversionfactor.html) to compute the conversion factor between...  bar to Pa, atm to Pa, bar to psi, and atm to psi.
bar2Pa = unitConversionFactor(units.Pa,units.Pa) %[control:editfield:0398]{"position":[31,39]} %[control:editfield:4555]{"position":[40,48]}
%[text]     b) atmosphere (atm) and Pascals (Pa)
atm2Pa = unitConversionFactor(units.Pa,units.Pa) %[control:editfield:376d]{"position":[31,39]} %[control:editfield:5d6e]{"position":[40,48]}
%[text]     c) bar (bar)  and pound per square inch (psi)
bar2psi = unitConversionFactor(units.Pa,units.Pa) %[control:editfield:7cb8]{"position":[32,40]} %[control:editfield:2285]{"position":[41,49]}
%[text]     d) atmosphere (atm) and pound per square inch (psi)
atm2psi = unitConversionFactor(units.Pa,units.Pa) %[control:editfield:0754]{"position":[32,40]} %[control:editfield:5363]{"position":[41,49]}

  %[control:button:5fc0]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckUnitConversionFactor(bar2Pa, atm2Pa, bar2psi, atm2psi);
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:0406) **Try.** Explore all units of pressure available. Feel free to explore more units, such as mH2O and mmHg, on your own.
% You can used this space to try computing any other pressure conversion factor:

%%
%[text] So far, we have learned about the different units of pressure, how to define them, and how to compute the conversion factor from one unit to another. MATLAB can rewrite any symbolic expression expressed in one unit into another compatible unit. Two compatible units are units that are linked by a dimensionless conversion factor. For example, meter (m) and Fahrenheit (F) are two incompatible units, while inch (in) and meter (m) are compatible units.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:5379) **Try.** Convert inches to meters.
inchesInMeter = rewrite(units.in, units.m)
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:9f69) **Exercise.** Use [`rewrite`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/rewrite.html?searchHighlight=rewrite&s_tid=srchtitle_rewrite_1) to convert Pascals into Newtons and meters.
Pascals = 0;
  %[control:button:96f8]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckDecompositionUnits(Pascals);
%[text] 
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:6b60) **Reflect**. Can you link the answer for the previous exercise and [this equation](internal:M_39947CDA) from the introduction? 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_93417143] ## Bernoulli's Principle
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_53880FAF] ### Definition
%[text] The Bernoulli equation is a fundamental equation of fluid dynamics that describes the relationship between pressure, velocity, and elevation change in the fluid. This equation is named after the Swiss mathematician Daniel Bernoulli, who formulated it in 1738. The Bernoulli equation states that the total pressure of a fluid system is constant along a [streamline](internal:M_1D25B18A) with the total pressure defined by:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_4216247D] $P\_\\text{total}= P+\n\\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2+\n\\rho g z \\qquad(\\star)${"altText":"P\_\text{total}= P+ \frac{1}{2}\rho v^2+ \rho g z \qquad(\star)"}
%[text] where $P${"altText":"P"} represents the static pressure of the fluid, $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"} its density, $v${"altText":"v"} its velocity, $g${"altText":"g"} gravitational acceleration, $z${"altText":"z"} the elevation and:
%[text] - $\\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2${"altText":"\frac{1}{2}\rho v^2"} is the dynamic pressure. It is the pressure that is due to the bulk motion of the fluid.
%[text] - $\\rho g z${"altText":"\rho g z"} It is the hydrostatic pressure; it is the pressure that is due to the gravitational potential. The component is important in a fluid system with liquid because of its high density and is often neglected with gases. \
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_1D25B18A] **Streamline:** In fluid dynamics, the mathematical definition of a streamline is the flow line tangent to the velocity vector at every point. Physically, it represents the path that a small, frictionless particle would follow if it were to flow in this fluid.
%[text] We should verify that each term of Bernoulli's equation is consistent with a unit of pressure.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:08e6) **Try.** Check if the dynamic pressure term is consistent as a unit of pressure.
units = symunit;
DynamicPressureUnits = units.kg/units.m^3*(units.m/units.s)^2;
checkUnits(DynamicPressureUnits == units.Pa)
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:3ff7) **Exercise.** Similarly, check that the hydrostatic pressure term is consistent as a unit of pressure.
% Your code goes in the next line:
HydrostaticPressureUnits = 0;

% Check the units consistency:
  checkUnits(HydrostaticPressureUnits == units.Pa) %[control:button:9387]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:8da5) **Reflect**. Reflect on the similarity between the term in equation $(\\star)${"altText":"(\star)"} and the definition of work, kinetic energy, and potential energy.
%[text] - Work: $W = \\int P dV${"altText":"W = \int P dV"}
%[text] - Kinetic energy: $E\_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2${"altText":"E\_k = \frac{1}{2}mv^2"}
%[text] - Potential energy: $E\_p = mgz${"altText":"E\_p = mgz"} \
%%
%[text] The Bernoulli equation $(\\star)${"altText":"(\star)"} is often written in terms of hydraulic head $H${"altText":"H"}:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $H = \\frac{P}{\\rho g} + \\frac{v^2}{2 g} + z\n\\quad\\quad\n(\\spadesuit)${"altText":"H = \frac{P}{\rho g} + \frac{v^2}{2 g} + z \quad\quad (\spadesuit)"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_294DEE77] ### **Pressure along a streamline**
%[text] 
%[text] We are considering the streamline of a free-flowing fluid between point A and point B. The velocity at points A and B and the elevation change are known. We will express the pressure in relative pressure, and the reference pressure is equal to the absolute pressure at point A, so its relative pressure is 0. We are trying to compute the pressure at point B.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:5818) **Try.** Use the slider below to visualize a streamline between points A and B of a fluid flow with an elevation difference.
% Select a fluid:
rho = 1000*units.kg/units.m^3; %[control:dropdown:3884]{"position":[7,11]}

% Point A data:
VelocityA = 5* units.m/units.s; %[control:slider:5e42]{"position":[13,14]}
PressureA = 0*units.Pa;

% Point B data:
VelocityB = 5*units.m/units.s; %[control:slider:57bc]{"position":[13,14]}

% Elevation change and gravity:
dh = 2* units.m; %[control:slider:2b85]{"position":[6,7]}
g = 9.81*units.m/units.s^2;

  %[control:button:05da]{"position":[1,2]}
PlotStreamline(dh,VelocityA,VelocityB)
%%
%[text] We use the symbolic math toolbox to define the total pressure at points A and B as a function of the problem variable.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:7fd9) **Exercise.** Write the total pressure at points A and B.
syms PressureB

% Your code goes here:
TotalPressureA = 0;
TotalPressureB = 0;
  CheckFreeStreamBernoulli(TotalPressureA,TotalPressureB,PressureA,VelocityA,VelocityB,rho,g,dh) %[control:button:12ac]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] We can define the equality of the total pressure and solve for the pressure at point B.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:9039) **Try.** [Solve](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/sym.solve.html) the pressure at point B using the equation $(\\star)${"altText":"(\star)"}.
% Define the equation to solve:
Eqn = TotalPressureB == TotalPressureA;
PressureB = solve(Eqn, PressureB)
  %[control:button:1fc3]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:8e2a) **Try.** Rewrite the pressure in your unit of choice.
% Select your choise of pressure:
UnitChoice = units.psi; %[control:dropdown:0785]{"position":[20,23]}

% Rewrite the upstream and downstream pressure in that unit:
PressureB = vpa(rewrite(PressureB, UnitChoice))
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6AA19CFF] ### **Application: Converging Pipe Flow**
%[text] 
%[text] We just considered the flow along the streamline of a free-flowing fluid. In this section, we consider the flow in a converging pipe of upstream diameter $D\_A${"altText":"D\_A"} and downstream diameter $D\_B${"altText":"D\_B"}; the inflow velocity is $V\_A${"altText":"V\_A"}, and the fluid is incompressible.
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Illustration of converging pipe with upstream and downstream diameter annotation](text:image:7fe9)
%[text] 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:58c9) **Exercise.** Considering that the mass is conserved in the pipe, express the velocity at point B as a function of the velocity at point A and the diameters.
% Define the problem parameters: 
syms DiameterA DiameterB VelocityA PressureA PressureB

% You code goes here:
VelocityB = 0;

  %[control:button:60d4]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckVelocityConvergentPipe(VelocityB)
%%
%[text] Following the same step as the previous section, we can express the total pressure at points A and B.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:5600) **Exercise.** Considering no elevation change, express the total pressure at points A and B.
TotalPressureA = 0;
TotalPressureB = 0;

  %[control:button:6774]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckTotalPressureConvergentPipe(TotalPressureA, TotalPressureB)
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:6f4b) **Try.** Solve the equation for $\\Delta P = P\_B - P\_A${"altText":"\Delta P = P\_B - P\_A"}
PressureDifference = solve( TotalPressureA == TotalPressureB , PressureB ) - PressureA
PressureDifference = simplify(PressureDifference)
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:2d8a) **Reflect**. Knowing that $D\_A \> D\_B${"altText":"D\_A \> D\_B"} should we expect the pressure to increase or decrease along the converging pipe? This counter-intuitive result is referred to as Bernoulli's principle.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_21C10CA2] ### **Application: Pitot tube**
%[text] 
%[text] A pitot tube is a device used to measure velocity with a fluid that is particularly used in aviation. It usually consists of a thin hollow tube pointed toward the fluid flow. For example, in the following figure, the NH-90's pitot tube is oriented toward the airplane's cockpit. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Picture of a Pitot tube on a NH-90 helicopter](text:image:6946)
%[text]{"align":"center"} An NH-90's pitot tube, [Wikimedia](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NH90_(NH-211)_Kokonaisturvallisuus_2015_03_pitot_tube.JPG)
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] There are multiple types of Pitot tubes. In this script, we are studying a Pitot-static tube. This device presents two openings, one in the front and one on the side. One of those openings is used to define the stagnation pressure:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_\\text{stagnation} = P\_\\text{static} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2 ${"altText":"P\_\text{stagnation} = P\_\text{static} + \frac{1}{2}\rho v^2 "}
%[text] This pressure represents the pressure the fluid would have if it came to a sudden stop. The following figure presents a schematic representation of the Pitot-static tube with the pressure gauge.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Illustration of a Pitot-static tube setup](text:image:9dea)
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:82f2) **Exercise.** Identify the kind of pressure that is measured by each of the pressure gauges.
CheckPressurePitot(GaugeA="None",GaugeB="None"); %[control:dropdown:86ce]{"position":[27,33]} %[control:dropdown:6398]{"position":[41,47]}
  %[control:button:9213]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] Now that both pressures are known, we are considering the difference in pressure between the two gauges:  $\\Delta P = P\_B - P\_A${"altText":"\Delta P = P\_B - P\_A"}
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:575e) **Exercise.** Based on your answer to the previous question, what pressure is equal to $\\Delta P = P\_B - P\_A${"altText":"\Delta P = P\_B - P\_A"} represents?
CheckPressureDifferencePitot("None"); %[control:dropdown:03dd]{"position":[30,36]}
  %[control:button:97d8]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:1523) **Exercise.** Express the free-stream velocity  `freeStreamVelocity` as a function of $\\Delta P= P\_B - P\_A${"altText":"\Delta P= P\_B - P\_A"}
% Declare the parameters of the problem:
syms StaticPressure rho AirVelocity

% Your code goes here:
PressureA = 0;
PressureB = 0;

% Compute the pressure difference:
PressureDifference = PressureB - PressureA
  %[control:button:6742]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckPressureDiffPitot(PressureDifference)
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:319f) **Try.** Plot the pressure difference as a function of the airspeed at a density of 1.14 kg/m3 for airspeed from 0 to 100 m/s.
% Plot the results if the previous exercise was completed:
if PressureDifference ~= 0
    figure
    PressureDifference(airVelocity) = subs(PressureDifference, rho, 1.14);
    fplot(PressureDifference,[0 100],LineWidth=2)
    title("Static Pitot tube reading")
    ylabel("Pressure difference (Pa)")
    xlabel("Airpseed (m/s)")
end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0189E81A] ### 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9FA8DB31] ### **Application: Lift generation around an airfoil**
%[text] 
%[text] We are studying the lift generation around an airfoil applied to a NACA-2412 airfoil. The NACA airfoils are a series of wing profiles developed by the National Advisory Committee for Aeronautics (NACA) in the 1920s and 1930s. These airfoils have been thoroughly studied and have been the basis of airfoil design for decades, and are still used today. The NACA-2412 is a wing profile with a max camber of 2%, located at 40% of the chords with a thickness of 12%, hence 2412. Each NACA airfoil is defined by 4 or 5 digits representing the geometrical features of the airfoil.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:12a0) **Try.** Plot the NACA-2412 profile.
  %[control:button:396f]{"position":[1,2]}
[~,~,~] = NACA4digits(12,2,40);
%[text] For more information about the NACA airfoil, please visit this module's documentation of the `NACA4digits` function.
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] doc NACA4digits
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] The NACA-2412 is a common airfoil used for small aircraft and control surfaces like the [Cessna-172](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cessna_172). It is usually used for applications with relatively low airspeed, high lift, and low drag, around 120 knots. Airfoils obstruct the flow, and when the aircraft is at a certain airspeed, the air velocity above and below the airfoil is slightly different. We can define the velocity over and under the airfoil as follows:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $V=V\_\\text{cruise}+V'${"altText":"V=V\_\text{cruise}+V'"}
%[text] where, $V${"altText":"V"} is the velocity, $V\_\\text{cruise}${"altText":"V\_\text{cruise}"} is the cruise velocity of the aircraft, and $V'${"altText":"V'"} is the difference in velocity. There is a streamline going from downstream to the upper part of the airfoil and another going from downstream to the lower part of the airfoil. Therefore, we can write the Bernoulli equations between those points:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_\\text{total,downstream}=P\_\\text{total,upper}=P\_\\text{total,lower}${"altText":"P\_\text{total,downstream}=P\_\text{total,upper}=P\_\text{total,lower}"}
%[text] Hence,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_\\text{upper} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho V\_\\text{upper}=P\_\\text{lower} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho V\_\\text{lower}${"altText":"P\_\text{upper} + \frac{1}{2}\rho V\_\text{upper}=P\_\text{lower} + \frac{1}{2}\rho V\_\text{lower}"}
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:9faa) **Exercise.** Write the total pressure over and under the airfoil.
% Set the problem unknown:
syms UpperVelocity LowerVelocity UpperPressure LowerPressure rho

% Write your code here:
UpperTotalPressure = 0;
LowerTotalPressure = 0;

% Define the pressure difference:
PressureDifference = solve(UpperTotalPressure == LowerTotalPressure, UpperPressure) - LowerPressure
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:5094) **Try.** Set a larger velocity along the lower surface than along the upper surfaces.
% Set the value:
ValueRho = 1.14;
ValueUpperVelocity = 50; %[control:slider:42c9]{"position":[22,24]}
ValueLowerVelocity = 50; %[control:slider:2298]{"position":[22,24]}

% Rewrite the results:
PressureDifferenceValue = subs(PressureDifference, [rho UpperVelocity LowerVelocity],[ValueRho ValueUpperVelocity ValueLowerVelocity])
%[text] 
%%
%[text] The difference in pressure over and below the airfoil creates a force that applies vertically to the airfoil.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:9f12) **Exercise.** Should the pressure difference be positive or negative to generate an upward "lift" force along the airfoil?
CheckPressureDifferenceSign("None") %[control:dropdown:0112]{"position":[29,35]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7ED8B157] ## Further Exploration
%[text] 
%[text] - In this script, we only considered ideal flow where the total pressure is conserved along streamline. In all real flow, friction introduces pressure drop. Therefore, the total pressure decreases along a streamline. These pressure losses, often called head losses, can be modeled. Explore [InternalFlow.m](file:./InternalFlow.m) to learn more about realistic flow applied to house plumbing. \
